preConditionHook:
// Brooks: 'the bull trend bars were clearly more prominent than the bear trend
// bars in the range' -- read as a COUNT difference, one side's tally minus the
// other's against a margin. Structurally TailBarCountDominance with trend bars
// substituted for tail bars, and its counted-series handling is copied here.
//
// Not TrendBarCountInWindow, which tests one side against an ABSOLUTE count
// and says nothing about the other side. Not BullBearBodyDominanceInWindow,
// which weighs the same idea by body SIZE: three small bull bars beat two big
// bear bars here and not there.
//
// The EasyLanguage twin uses CountIF, which counts bars 0..N-1 INCLUDING the
// current bar and whose argument is a true SERIES whose offsets shift with the
// counted bar (EL_FEATURES `countif`, VERIFIED). Every operand is a raw price
// at offset 0, so the loop below is that count bar for bar. Prices have real
// history back to the chart start, so no zero-filled variable mirror is
// needed. The counts are EL numeric variables, kept as doubles.
//
// The body test is a product, never a quotient: EL raises a runtime
// divide-by-zero and a zero-range bar is legal data.
bullTrendBars = 0.0;
bearTrendBars = 0.0;
for (int i = 0; i < (int)lookback; ++i) {
    if (el_gt(close[i], open[i])
        && el_ge(close[i] - open[i], bodyFraction * (high[i] - low[i]))) {
        bullTrendBars += 1;
    }
    if (el_lt(close[i], open[i])
        && el_ge(open[i] - close[i], bodyFraction * (high[i] - low[i]))) {
        bearTrendBars += 1;
    }
}
